    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d516">

                <pb facs="0554" n="388" sameAs="frus:frus1917/pg388"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 837.00/1270</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minister Gonzales to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 428</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-03-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 23, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit in duplicate
                    a communication dated March 20 from the commanding officer of the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Eagle</hi> to the Secretary of the Navy, via American
                    Minister, Habana. Attached to the first copy are a memorandum signed by several
                    rebel officers who conferred with an officer of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Eagle</hi> at Manati, and two petitions for protection from American
                    residents of Bartle<anchor n="1" xml:id="d516-f1"/> and Galbis.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d516-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>The commander of the <hi rendition="#italic">Eagle</hi> states that he “feels
                    certain that if the United States sees fit to act favorably” on the request of
                    the rebels, as conveyed in this memorandum, for action by the United States
                    which would in effect be a recognition of the justice of the rebellion and
                    admission of the Government’s defeat, “quiet and order will be quickly
                    restored.” But while apparently urging a course calculated to quell the
                    immediate disorder, he does not seem to consider the effect which justification
                    of the rebellion might have on the future peace of Cuba.</p>
                <p>I have communicated to the Foreign Office the requests of the American citizens
                    resident at Bartle and Galbis for protection.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William E. Gonzales</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d516-a1.xml" xpointer="d516-a1"/>
